    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 241958\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n1258 PM MST Sat Oct 24 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Below normal temperatures will continue through the weekend \n  before readings warm back closer to the seasonal average for the\n  bulk of next week.\n\n- Aside from a few light showers today, dry and tranquil weather \n  conditions will grace the region through the middle of next \n  week.\n\n- A return to inclement weather with increased rainfall chances \n  is possible towards the end of next week. \n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH SUNDAY/... \n\nThe cut off low system is quickly moving through the lower \ndeserts, with the core of the low now over Maricopa County and is \nexpected to lift to the N/NE into northern AZ and over the Four \nCorners region by Sunday afternoon, and exiting by Monday. As this\nsystem passes very localized areas across Maricopa and Gila \nCounty can expect a few isolated showers through the afternoon, \nwith a few locations in the metro already experiencing sprinkles \nover them, activity is then expected to clear and lift north of \nthe CWA by late this afternoon. For the higher terrain areas, \nthere also remains a low chance of an isolated thunderstorm \ndeveloping this afternoon. By Sunday, dry conditions are expected\nunder mostly clear skies, thanks to H5 heights increasing to \nbetween 576-579 dam, resulting in afternoon high temperatures \nincreasing 2-3 degrees from today. This warming trend will \ncontinue into the beginning of the week, but thankfully over the \nweekend the region will remain near to slight below normal, in the\nupper 70s to lower 80s.\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/... \n\nGradually warming temperatures and overall tranquil weather is\nexpected through at least the first half of the next workweek with\nquasi-zonal flow in the wake of the weekend low. An increase in H5\nheights up to 579-582 dam by Monday will support the gradual\nwarming of temperatures, with afternoon highs reaching the middle\n80s and lows in the upper 50s to lower 60s across the lower\ndeserts, which is right around normal for the time of year. Dry \nmostly clear skies will prevail under this pattern as well. Model \nuncertainty grows later next week as there are hints at some \nmoisture return into the region from the southeast, then a \npotential atmospheric river approaching by next weekend. Either, \nor both of these moisture sources may eventually help to bring a \nreturn to rain chances at some point before the end of the month, \nbut it is too early to have a good idea on timing and/or placement\nfor any weather feature this far in advance. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1720Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \n\nNo major aviation concerns are expected during the TAF period.\nWinds will follow familiar diurnal trends with speeds generally\naob 10 kt. A very isolated SHRA cannot be ruled out this\nafternoon, but chances of this occurring are 10% or less, so \nthere is currently no mention on the TAFs. If VCSH do come to \nfruition, some brief erratic winds will be the main impact. SCT \ncloud decks, around 7-9 kft will be present through this evening \nbefore clear skies take over from there.\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nNo aviation concerns are expected over the next 24 hours. Winds\nwill favor a W'rly component at KIPL, while KBLH will see N'rly\nflow tilt more to the west by this evening. Periodic windows of \nVRB conditions will be observed during the forecast window. Clear \nskies will be common through Sunday morning.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nA low pressure system will linger across the region this weekend \nbringing light shower chances this afternoon, but little to no \nwetting rainfall is expected. Minimum humidity levels will fall \ninto a 25-50% range this afternoon and then dry heading into the \nstart of the workweek, with minimum humidity falling closer to \n15-35%. Overnight recoveries will remain good at 45-70% across the\narea. Overall quiet weather conditions are forecast Sunday \nthrough at least the first half of next week, with temperatures \nrebounding back into the normal range by Monday, and with light \nwinds each day favoring typical diurnal tendencies. A shift in the\nweather pattern late next week and into next week may bring about\ncooler, wetter, and breezier conditions.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Ryan\nLONG TERM...18/Benedict \nAVIATION...RW \nFIRE WEATHER...Ryan/Kuhlman\n",
